---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PdfSecureMarkdownToWord from '@/components/tools/data/PdfSecureMarkdownToWord.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="text-to-word">
  <PdfSecureMarkdownToWord client:load source="text" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>Text to Word, with real paragraphs</h2>
      <p>
        Word opens a <code>.txt</code> file as one long unstyled block, with every hard line break kept as a
        real break, and you must reflow it by hand. This page does that work for you.
      </p>
      <p>
        The output is a real .docx with paragraph objects, so text wraps to the page, spacing is consistent,
        and the document behaves like one written in Word.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The conversion rules</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">In your text</th>
              <th scope="col">Becomes</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>A blank line</td>
              <td>A new paragraph</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>A single line break inside a block</td>
              <td>Joined into flowing text, or kept if you tick the option</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>A short ALL-CAPS line on its own</td>
              <td>Heading 2 (optional)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>A block where every line starts with -, * or •</td>
              <td>A real Word bulleted list</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Windows or classic Mac line endings</td>
              <td>Normalised; CRLF and CR both work</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Tabs</td>
              <td>Kept inside the paragraph text</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>When to keep every line break</h2>
      <p>
        By default the tool joins lines within a paragraph. This is right for prose hard-wrapped at a fixed
        column width, such as the 72-character convention of email and README files. Turn the option on when
        the line endings have a meaning:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Poetry and song lyrics, where the line is the unit.</li>
        <li>Postal addresses and letterheads.</li>
        <li>Log excerpts, stack traces and console output.</li>
        <li>Scripts and screenplays.</li>
        <li>Text already laid out with deliberate alignment.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        With the option on, each line becomes a soft break inside one paragraph, not a separate paragraph.
        This keeps the spacing tight and lets you style the whole block as one unit.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The ALL-CAPS heading rule</h2>
      <p>
        Plain-text documents used capitals for section titles long before word processors. The tool treats a
        line as a heading when all of these are true:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>it stands alone, with no other lines in its block;</li>
        <li>it is 80 characters or fewer;</li>
        <li>it does not end in <code>.</code> <code>:</code> <code>;</code> <code>,</code> <code>!</code> or <code>?</code>;</li>
        <li>it contains at least one letter and is entirely in capitals.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Turn the rule off if your text uses capitals for other reasons, such as product codes, legal
        disclaimers or chat logs. The block counts under the result show how many headings the tool found
        before you open the file.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Encoding and special characters</h2>
      <p>
        Files are read as UTF-8, so accented Latin, Greek, Cyrillic, CJK and emoji all go into the .docx
        unchanged. A Word document does not embed fonts by default; it refers to them by name, and the
        reader's installed fonts render the text. For this reason full Unicode works here, while the PDF tools
        on this site are limited to their built-in fonts.
      </p>
      <p>
        Control characters other than tab and newline are removed, because Word refuses files that contain
        them.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Good sources for this converter</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Notes from a plain-text editor that you must now share.</li>
        <li>A transcript or dictation export.</li>
        <li>Output from a command-line tool that must go into a report.</li>
        <li>An old <code>.txt</code> archive for a document management system.</li>
        <li>Text copied from a terminal, a chat window or a code comment.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If the text uses Markdown (<code>#</code> headings, <code>**bold**</code>, pipe tables), use
        <a href="/markdown-to-word">Markdown to Word</a>. For a PDF, use <a href="/text-to-pdf">Text to
        PDF</a>. To check the length before you convert, use the <a href="/word-counter">Word counter</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
